      "writtenOn": "2023-07-07T15:00:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The size is small because it is only meant for testing purposes. I just want to print something to be sure that it works.\n\nTypically, the size of the seed should be at least as large as the size of the random array that the PRNG is supposed to generate. If the seed size is smaller than the size of the random array, the PRNG may produce patterns or cycles in the generated random numbers.\n\nThe `RAND_bytes()` function checks that the PRNG is seeded with sufficient randomness, as documented in [1].\n\n[1] https://www.openssl.org/docs/man1.0.2/man3/RAND_bytes.html",
      "parentUuid": "9132301f_ad742d0b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 72,
        "startChar": 37,
        "endLine": 72,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "d5879d7e72f397ad8a06d4556beeeea440c96233",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "55c49a8a_2a9e2eff",
        "filename": "vmbase/example/src/main.rs",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 72,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-07-07T15:34:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Typically, the size of the seed should be at least as large as the size of the random array that the PRNG is supposed to generate.\n\nWait, this confuses me:\n\n1. Doesn\u0027t a PRNG work by seeding it once with a seed (order of magnitude: tens of bytes) before using it to produce _any_ amount of random bytes (potentially Gigs of data)?\n1. If the PRNG requires as many bytes of seed as it produces of pseudo-entropy then what\u0027s the point of the PRNG? Cryptographically, why not use the entropy contained in the seed in the first place? And, in our particular case of `vmbase`, what are we gaining from adding support for a PRNG if it doesn\u0027t reduce the pressure on the TRNG?\n\n\u003e [1] https://www.openssl.org/docs/man1.0.2/man3/RAND_bytes.html\n\nDo we want to expose both `RAND_bytes` and `RAND_pseudo_bytes` to clients? Are those actually mapping to TRNG and PRNG, respectively? If we keep the seed secret from the host, isn\u0027t `RAND_pseudo_bytes` enough for generating the DICE salt stored in the instance.img?",
      "parentUuid": "27e6e86c_67e90260",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 72,
        "startChar": 37,
        "endLine": 72,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "d5879d7e72f397ad8a06d4556beeeea440c96233",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "31f1b6fe_2a28e399",
        "filename": "vmbase/example/src/main.rs",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 72,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-07-10T12:18:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Doesn\u0027t a PRNG work by seeding it once with a seed (order of magnitude: tens of bytes) before using it to produce _any_ amount of random bytes (potentially Gigs of data)?\n\nA PRNG generates a sequence of numbers that appear to be random, but it is not truly random because it operates deterministically. It works like this:\n\nSeed -\u003e (initialize) -\u003e state_1 -\u003e (update) -\u003e state_2 -\u003e (update) -\u003e ...\n\nMathematically, a discrete PRNG cannot produce an infinite amount of random bytes, because the PRNG operates on a finite state space {0,1}^N. If the state space is small, the cycle will show up quickly. However, in practice, the state space is usually sufficiently large[1] to avoid repetition for Gigs of data.\n\nRegarding the seed, if the seed is too small, one can enumerate all the possibilities, making it vulnerable to brute-force attacks[2]. Especially if the entropy used to generate the seed is extracted from a single source, since this may result in a non-uniform distribution over the seed space {0,1}^S. It is generally recommended to have at least 32 bytes seed as a starting point [2][3]. As the seed length is critical for PRNG, it\u0027s common to have it as big as the output length [4].\n\n[1] https://www.openssl.org/docs/man1.0.2/man3/rand.html\n[2] https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/339.html\n[3] https://wiki.openssl.org/index.php/Random_Numbers \u003e Seeds\n[4] https://www.nist.gov/publications/recommendation-random-number-generation-using-deterministic-random-bit-generators-2 \u003e 8.6.4 \u0026 10\n\n\u003e what are we gaining from adding support for a PRNG if it doesn\u0027t reduce the pressure on the TRNG?\n\nPRNG is just seeded once with the entropy source from TRNG. It is more efficient than TRNG for our use case.\n\n\u003e Do we want to expose both `RAND_bytes` and `RAND_pseudo_bytes` to clients? Are those actually mapping to TRNG and PRNG, respectively?\n\nNot exactly. I don\u0027t think we need `RAND_pseudo_bytes` for now. Both of them are PRNG. Based on the spec [3][4]\n\u003e `RAND_bytes` puts num cryptographically strong pseudo-random bytes into buf. `RAND_pseudo_bytes` puts num pseudo-random bytes into buf.\n\nSo `RAND_pseudo_bytes` is to be used for non-cryptographic purposes such as statistical sampling.\n\n[4] https://www.openssl.org/docs/man1.0.2/man3/RAND_bytes.html",
      "parentUuid": "55c49a8a_2a9e2eff",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 72,
        "startChar": 37,
        "endLine": 72,
        "endChar": 39
      },
